The aging of the population has increased the demand for systems capable of
assisting in the monitoring of people who require continuous care. In this
context, we present the development of a system based on Deep Learning
models for sentiment analysis using images and videos, focusing on pain
detection. The system was developed in Python, using the OpenCV library
for frame capture and the vision models for Visual Question Answering
and Image-Text Matching tasks. Initially, the presence of people in the
images is identified,and then the sentiment classification is determined
between the classes "with pain" and "without pain". For performance
evaluation, a database composed of 181 images was used
(https://github.com/fabiorcerqueira/pain_nopain_img_rep). This
images were validated through Google Forms (scripts in
https://github.com/fabiorcerqueira/create_form_for_img_human_validation).
The proposed framework/system may assist professionals and caregivers
in the early identification of risk situations involving people with special
needs. This work also contributes to the testing of various models for
the sentiment detection purpose and the testing of propmt sensitivity. It
is possible to reproduce the experiments and check the statistics by
accessing the zip file "Florence_supplement_for_reproducibility.zip"
in this repository.
